{"id":605,"date":"2020-10-29T17:56:39","date_gmt":"2020-10-30T00:56:39","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=605"},"modified":"2024-08-30T10:03:02","modified_gmt":"2024-08-30T17:03:02","slug":"keyboard-remote-control","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/keyboard-remote-control\/","title":{"rendered":"1G: Keyboard Remote Control"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213f3e2016\" name=\"6a2213f3e2016\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Getting Started<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Make sure that your CoDrone Mini is paired before starting the lesson! If you forgot how to do this, make sure to review how to pair to Blockly <a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/cdmb-pairing\/\">here<\/a>.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213f3e21c8\" name=\"6a2213f3e21c8\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Practice program<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You might be thinking, \u201cBut Robolink! There\u2019s a remote control with my CoDrone Mini! Can\u2019t I just learn how to use that!\u201d The answer is yes, but in a different lesson. In this lesson, you will get to apply everything you\u2019ve learned so far into one big program.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Just to see how exactly this works, you\u2019re going to program your drone to take off, and then when you hit a key (we picked t), it will turn to the left. When you hit the spacebar, it will land. First, drag a take off block to your workspace.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213f3e224b\" name=\"6a2213f3e224b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Forever loop<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Since you want your program to constantly check if you are pressing a key, you will need to use a forever loop. For this example, use a while loop block and connect an &#8220;is code running?&#8221; block from the orange status checkers menu.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;606&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213f3e2301\" name=\"6a2213f3e2301\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Add a conditional<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now it&#8217;s time to check for keyboard input! Since your program needs to make a decision, you should use a conditional. Go to the logic menu and drag a conditional block to your workspace. <\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;609&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, go to the light gray keyboard input menu and attach a when backspace key press block to your conditional. Be careful! There are two, so make sure to use the one that looks like a puzzle piece on the left.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;611&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Change backspace to t using the dropdown menu, and then add a turn left block from the flight directions menu.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;614&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_single_image image=&#8221;615&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213f3e236d\" name=\"6a2213f3e236d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Adding more keys<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To add the spacebar key for your drone to land, click on the gear icon in the conditional block and attach an else if block to the if block.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;618&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, go back to the keyboard input menu, select the same when backspace key press block, and attach it to the else statement. Select spacebar from the drop down menu.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;619&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Go to the flight directions menu and attach a land block to your program.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;620&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213f3e23ce\" name=\"6a2213f3e23ce\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Keyboard remote control<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now that you have the basic idea, you can start making a keyboard remote control! Start a new file and set up the structure of the statements you will use for forward, backward, left, right, and emergency stop.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, add in your flight commands. With this remote control, you will move in a direction for half a second. If the spacebar is pressed, the drone should emergency stop and exit out of the forever loop. If no key is being pressed, the drone should hover. For this last part, you should use an else statement.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">We picked keys that matched our keys to the direction (like u for up), but you can use whatever keys you like. However, they should be something that\u2019s easy for you to remember so you don\u2019t use the wrong key. Using backspace might clear your code, so that should be avoided.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;621&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213f3e2426\" name=\"6a2213f3e2426\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Add more commands<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If you want your drone to turn or fly forward or backward, you just need to go back to the gear icon in the conditional block and add as many else if blocks. Next, add your keyboard input and flight direction blocks.\u00a0<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;622&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213f3e2541\" name=\"6a2213f3e2541\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Activity: Obstacle course<\/b><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now that you have your new flight controller, build an obstacle course and try to fly to the finish line without hitting anything! If you need to, adjust the values of the delays and add more keys for more functionality!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 9 Getting Started [vc_column_text]Make sure that your CoDrone Mini is paired before starting the lesson! If you forgot how to do this, make sure to review how to&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/keyboard-remote-control\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1687,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":true,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":407,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1814,1760,1817,1763,1777,1737,1815,1793,626,1533,1438,853,144,167,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2108,2125,2124,2121,2122,2111],"class_list":["post-605","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-blockly","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-junior","lesson-tag-keyboard","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-remote-control","module-junior","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/605","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":13,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/605\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":12816,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/605\/revisions\/12816"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1687"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=605"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=605"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=605"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}